==Traffic generator LiteX CSRs==

The wrapper exposes the core's registers directly as named LiteX CSRs:
id, version, flip, control, interframe_gap, interburst_gap, frames_per_burst,
total_frames (64 bits), pkts (64 bits), frame_size, frame_buf and
frame_buf_address. There is no software AXI transaction protocol.

Each board target currently enables traffic_generator_gmii_0 under crg.
Its generated csr.csv/csr.h prefix is crg_traffic_generator_gmii_0_.
The shared helper supports additional numbered instances when requested.
For example, configure and read instance 0:

 crg_traffic_generator_gmii_0_interframe_gap_write(12);
 crg_traffic_generator_gmii_0_frame_size_write(72);
 crg_traffic_generator_gmii_0_frame_buf_address_write(0);
 crg_traffic_generator_gmii_0_frame_buf_write(0x11223344);
 crg_traffic_generator_gmii_0_total_frames_write(100);
 crg_traffic_generator_gmii_0_control_write(1);
 uint64_t sent = crg_traffic_generator_gmii_0_pkts_read();

Use the generated addresses and accessors: LiteX allocates CSR addresses and
splits wide registers according to its configured CSR width and ordering.
The old AXI byte offsets are not used as LiteX CSR offsets. Reads of writable
configuration CSRs return their stored configuration; flip reads return the
core's inverted value. pkts is the live core counter; multiword reads are not
atomic. Each frame_buf write stores a word and advances the internal address.
Writing frame_size resets that address; frame_buf_address sets it explicitly
(low 9 bits), and its CSR readback is the last value written.

The Verilog core selects the native register connection with C_USE_LITEX_CSR=1.
Its default (0) retains the original AXI register block for other systems.
CSR storage and buffer-write strobes stay in sys. Instance 0's transmit
logic uses the scaled PCS GMII clock. Ordered control writes cross through
a small queue; status reads must allow time for the slower datapath to update.

Register tests (with Migen and LiteX available on PYTHONPATH):
 cd systems/litex
 python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v

==Traffic analyzer LiteX CSRs==

Each board target currently instantiates traffic_analyzer_gmii_0 under crg,
with generated prefix crg_traffic_analyzer_gmii_0_. It exposes
native configuration, packet/octet/error counters, capture timestamps,
frame_size, frame_buf, latency and sequence-error statistics. flip supports
the same inverted readback as the generator. control defaults to 1:
bit 0 enables analysis, and bit 1 freezes statistics and capture.

For capture reads, set frame_buf_address to the desired word index (low
9 bits), then read frame_buf. Unlike the AXI streaming read interface,
CSR reads do not advance the address; software selects each word explicitly.
The synchronous capture RAM settles before the following CSR read. Freeze
capture before reading a frame that must remain stable. Multiword counter
reads are not atomic; use the core's freeze control when appropriate.

The 320-bit testframe_filter_data and testframe_filter_mask CSRs directly
configure the parser's 40-byte filter. Use the addresses and word ordering
in generated csr.csv/csr.h when accessing these wide registers.

The analyzer's default Verilog mode retains AXI; its LiteX wrapper selects
C_USE_LITEX_CSR=1. GMII and sec/nsec inputs are exposed for board wiring.
Generator 0 feeds gmii_to_1000basex, whose serial output feeds
basex1000_to_gmii. Its recovered GMII output feeds analyzer 0. The shared
wrapper supplies scaled clocks derived from sys; there is no direct GMII bypass.
The board targets use CSR pages of at least 4 KiB to fit both generators and
both analyzers with an 8-bit CSR bus. Rebuild software using the newly
generated CSR addresses. The register tests simulate all four instances.

Generator 0 and analyzer 0 receive stable falling-edge snapshots of the shared
rtclock time before their respective GMII rising edges.

==Simple generator 0 to analyzer 0 register sequence==

See examples/gmii_loopback.c for a minimal on-CPU C example using generated
CSR accessors. Call gmii_loopback_test(): 0 means one valid packet received;
-1 means timeout or an unexpected count. It stops the generator, clears and
enables the analyzer, loads a complete 72-byte static wire frame (including
preamble and valid FCS), sets total_frames=1, and starts control=1. The
confirmation read is crg_traffic_analyzer_gmii_0_pkts_read(), expected to
be 1. The analyzer remains enabled so its counters can be inspected afterward.
The polling limit is finite rather than a calibrated wall-clock timeout.

==Shared board integration==

Traffic-core creation, instance 0 serial PCS/PMA loopback and time connections live
in gateware/network_tester_cores.py. Board targets call
network_tester_cores_add(self, platform, instances=N, sys_clk_freq=sys_clk_freq)
on the CRG and
network_tester_cores_configure_csrs(kwargs) before SoCCore initialization. Cores attach
directly to the CRG, preserving existing CSR prefixes and register order.
All four board targets enable one generator/analyzer pair (instance 0).

==OrangeCrab generator clock complementary output==

The local boards/platforms/gsd_orangecrab.py reserves N17 (PB6A, positive)
and M18 (PB6B, negative) as gmii_en_lvds. Both pads use LVCMOS33.
GPIO connector positions 0 and 1 are unavailable, and feather_serial is
empty because those pins are now reserved. Other connector indices remain
unchanged. The target drives N17 with the generator's transmit GMII clock and M18
with its inverse using two ordinary CMOS outputs. These are complementary
3.3 V signals, not LVDS levels. The two output paths can have skew.
The resource keeps its historical gmii_en_lvds name.

==Standalone 1000BASE-X transmit PCS/PMA==

lib/hw/lsi/cores/pcs_1000basex_tx contains a Verilog GMII-to-1000BASE-X
transmitter. Its top gmii_to_1000basex takes a 1.25 GHz bit clock and
returns a 125 MHz clock for the upstream GMII producer. It includes
8b/10b coding, ordered sets and a digital 10:1 PMA serializer. See that
core's README.md for clock/reset requirements, configuration and tests.
The standalone core documents nominal clock rates. Board integration now
uses the scaled serial loopback described below without changing sys.

==Standalone 100BASE-X transmit PCS/PMA==

lib/hw/lsi/cores/pcs_100basex_tx provides matching Verilog files for
100BASE-FX (default 4B/5B + NRZI) and an optional scrambled 100BASE-TX
digital transmit path with MLT-3 level outputs. Its input clock is 125 MHz
and its returned byte-wide producer clock is 12.5 MHz, for 100 Mbit/s data.
It is not standard 25 MHz nibble-wide MII. Board/system clocks are unchanged.
See the core README.md for instantiation, interfaces and tests.

==Standalone 10BASE-FL transmitter==

lib/hw/lsi/cores/pcs_10basefl_tx adds matching byte-front-end, Manchester
encoder and digital PMA files. gmii_to_10basefl takes a 20 MHz half-bit
clock and returns a 1.25 MHz byte clock. It preserves the complete frame,
serializes LSB-first at 10 Mbit/s, and generates 800 ns low-light start of
idle followed by 1 MHz optical idle. OUTPUT_INVERT controls the external
driver polarity. See its README.md for interface/reset requirements and
scope. The board/system clocks and pins are unchanged.

==Standalone oversampled 100BASE-X receiver==

lib/hw/lsi/cores/pcs_100basex_rx adds the receive counterpart for the
unscrambled 100BASE-FX NRZI line. basex_to_gmii uses a 500 MHz sample clock
by default (4x oversampling) to recover 125 Mbit/s code bits, detect /J/K/,
decode 4B/5B and generate byte-wide GMII-style data at about 12.5 MHz.
Both a recovered byte clock and sample-domain gmii_rx_ce are available.
It does not decode analog copper MLT-3 or acquire a TX scrambler sequence.
See its README.md for interfaces, CDR limitations, framing and tests.

==Standalone oversampled 1000BASE-X receiver==

lib/hw/lsi/cores/pcs_1000basex_rx uses the 100BASE-X receive structure
at the requested 5 GHz sampling clock (4x a 1.25 Gbit/s NRZ line).
basex1000_to_gmii adds comma alignment, 8b/10b and disparity checking,
idle/configuration decoding and 125 MHz GMII output. It includes both
recovered-clock and sample-domain clock-enable interfaces. No sampling
clock reduction or board/system-clock change is made. See the core README.md
for acquisition policy, diagnostics and tests.

==Scaled 1000BASE-X serial loopback in network_tester_cores==

Data path:
 traffic_generator_gmii_0 -> gmii_to_1000basex -> serial ->
 basex1000_to_gmii -> traffic_analyzer_gmii_0

The shared gateware/pcs_1000basex_loopback.py wrapper supplies:
 bit_clk_x4 = sys                  (48 MHz on default OrangeCrab)
 bit_clk = bit_clk_x4 / 4          (12 MHz)
 TX and recovered RX GMII clocks   (approximately 1.2 MHz)

The PCS algorithms retain their 4x oversampling ratio. The link operates at
48/5000 of the nominal rate: 12 Mbit/s serial and 9.6 Mbit/s byte data.
Other board targets keep their own sys frequencies and scale proportionally.
No external clock inputs, high-speed PLL or system-frequency change is needed.

The generator datapath runs on the TX GMII clock; the analyzer runs on the
recovered RX GMII clock. Generated clocks have asynchronous reset assertion
and synchronized release, including while the RX clock is stopped. Generator
run is gated until CDR/PCS lock and recovered reset have settled. A software
start request made during training is retained and starts automatically.

CSR names/order are preserved. Their storage remains in sys. Generator
frame-buffer writes and auto-increment address logic use the CSR clock;
reading the transmit RAM uses its slower datapath clock. Capture RAM reads
remain in sys. The control registers have 16-entry ordered crossing queues,
so a short disable/enable sequence is not lost between slow datapath edges.
Do not exceed the queue capacity; overflow is available as an HDL diagnostic.
Allow control writes to drain before reconfiguring an active frame buffer or
assuming counters have cleared. Live counter reads remain non-atomic; use
freeze and allow it to reach the analyzer before reading wide statistics.

Shared time is snapped on each GMII falling edge in sys and held stable for
the next datapath rising edge. These are snapshots, not simultaneous reads
of the running counter; there can be up to one byte-clock period of age.
rtclock itself stays on sys and its existing tick configuration is unchanged.
This integration does not calibrate physical time or latency measurements.

In serial_loopback mode on OrangeCrab, N17/M18 monitor the TX GMII clock and
its inverse (nominal 1.2 MHz), still as complementary LVCMOS33 outputs.
Generated-clock period constraints are installed using each target's sys rate.

Validation: 8-bit and 32-bit CSR simulations verify clock ratios, frame RAM,
valid-CRC reception over the entire serial path, coherent time snapshots and
ordered control restart/reset. Yosys hierarchy/structural checks pass after
flattening/optimizing unused interface logic. Full board place-and-route and
hardware operation have not been verified for this integration.

==Selectable network tester data paths==

All four board targets accept:
 --network-tester-mode gmii             direct generator-to-analyzer GMII on sys
 --network-tester-mode serial_loopback  internal serial loopback (default)
 --network-tester-mode serial_external  independent board serial TX and RX

The Python API is network_tester_cores_add(..., mode="...", serial_pads=pads).
Single-ended pads expose tx and rx. Differential pads expose tx.p/tx.n and
rx.p/rx.n: TX uses complementary CMOS outputs. RX uses a DifferentialInput,
or digital p & ~n when pads.complementary_rx is True.
An optional signal_detect pad controls receiver signal detection; otherwise
signal detection is asserted. Only pair 0 is connected; additional numbered
pairs remain available for custom wiring. CSR names and addresses are unchanged.
External TX starts independently of RX lock; the analyzer waits for RX lock.
Both serial modes retain bit_clk_x4=sys, bit_clk=sys/4 and GMII=sys/40.

OrangeCrab external mode has fixed default pin assignments:
 tx0_p = N17, tx0_n = M18: complementary LVCMOS33 outputs
 rx0_p = B8,  rx0_n = C8: complementary LVCMOS33 inputs (rx0 = p & ~n)
The internal PCS serial nets are named tx0 and rx0. In external mode the
N17/M18 clock monitor is replaced by serial TX. The other two modes keep the
clock monitor (sys for GMII mode, TX GMII clock for serial loopback).
Both pairs use the 3.3 V CMOS workaround. Bank 1 top-edge pins do not support
a native differential input in the ECP5 toolchain. This is not LVDS signaling;
an LVDS-level source needs external conversion before the CMOS receive pins.

Other boards require explicit free single-ended pins, for example:
 --network-tester-mode serial_external \
 --network-tester-serial-tx-pin <TX_PIN> --network-tester-serial-rx-pin <RX_PIN>
 --network-tester-serial-io-standard LVCMOS33
These options also override OrangeCrab's default pairs with single-ended pins.
Choose pins and an I/O standard appropriate to the board bank voltage.

OrangeCrab also exposes traffic_generator_gmii_0.cycle_start as the single-ended
tx1 output on B10 (LVCMOS33) in every mode, with or without SDRAM.

OrangeCrab exposes traffic_generator_gmii_0.gmii_en as the single-ended tx2
output on B9 (LVCMOS33) in every mode, with or without SDRAM.

==One-frame test over the LiteX serial BIOS console==

Requires Python 3 and pyserial. From systems/litex run:
 python3 examples/serial_one_frame.py --verbose

Defaults: /dev/ttyACM0, 115200 baud, and build/gsd_orangecrab/csr.csv.
Use --csr-csv <path> matching the loaded bitstream; register addresses and
CSR widths are read from that file. Supports 8/16/32-bit CSR data with 32-bit
alignment and big CSR word ordering. CPU memory byte order defaults to little
(VexRiscv); --cpu-byteorder big selects a big-endian CPU memory dump.
Close other serial terminal programs before running. The BIOS must be at its
litex> prompt. In serial_external mode, connect the TX/RX physical path first.
For OrangeCrab's complementary CMOS pairs, connect N17 to B8 and M18 to C8
when using an external cable loopback.

The test stops the generator, clears/enables analyzer 0, writes a 72-byte
static frame with valid preamble/SFD and FCS, sets total_frames=1, and starts
it using mem_write commands. It verifies configuration readbacks and polls
mem_read counters, accounting for the generator's existing packet count.
PASS requires one transmitted and one received packet, zero bad CRC packets,
and zero bad preamble packets. The generator is stopped on exit; analyzer
counters remain available. Exit status: 0 PASS, 1 packet validation failure,
2 serial/console/configuration error. --timeout controls packet polling;
--command-timeout controls each BIOS reply. Command characters are paced at
2 ms and each command waits for the next prompt; --char-delay adjusts pacing.

The serial test always prints commands with [TX] and incoming replies with
[RX] by default, including prompt synchronization and partial replies before
a timeout. --verbose remains accepted for compatibility.

==Continuous 64-octet traffic and live serial status==

From systems/litex:
 python3 examples/serial_continuous_traffic.py --quiet-console

This uses the same pyserial dependency, port (/dev/ttyACM0), baud rate,
csr.csv, and BIOS mem_write/mem_read transport as serial_one_frame.py.
Close other serial terminal programs and provide a working GMII/internal
serial/external physical loopback. The script clears/enables the analyzer,
loads a static frame, and enables continuous generation with total_frames=0,
frames_per_burst=0 and interframe_gap=20 (interburst_gap is also set to 20).
A 64-octet Ethernet frame includes its 4-octet FCS and excludes preamble/SFD;
the generator frame_size register is therefore 72 GMII octets. No timestamp
or sequence-number insertion is enabled, so the static FCS remains valid.

The status display shows elapsed monitoring time, TX/RX packet totals for
this run, approximate host-timed packet rates, RX frame Mbit/s (64 octets per
frame, excluding preamble/gap), CRC errors, preamble errors and reception
state. Live counters are read with upper-limb verification/retry to avoid
rollover artifacts. Counters are read sequentially over the serial console;
TX/RX totals are not simultaneous snapshots or an exact packet-loss measure.
Default logging includes every command and reply; --quiet-console leaves
only configuration/status messages. --interval sets the pause between reads
(default 1 second); serial command time adds to each monitoring interval.
--duration 30 stops after 30 monitoring seconds, otherwise monitoring continues
until Ctrl-C. Both cases stop the generator and retain analyzer counters.
Serial/configuration failures also attempt to stop the generator before exit.

==GateMate EVB 125 MHz reference-clock output on J8==

The colognechip_gatemate_evb target now always exports serdes_refclk_out on
IO_SB_A7 (P12), the GPIO/CLK1 pin connected to the optional J8 SMA path.
A separate economy-mode CC_PLL generates 125 MHz from the board's 10 MHz
oscillator. The system PLL and selected sys_clk_freq remain independent.
The GPIO output uses the bank's existing 2.5 V supply and fast slew.

For the documented EVB revisions 3.1/3.2, populate J8 and R67 (0 ohm) if
missing, and bridge JP9 pins 2-3. This connects J8 to IO_SB_A7 and leaves
oscillator X2 disconnected. Leave optional shunt resistor R65 unpopulated
for this GPIO-driven output unless the output loading has been designed
for it. The corresponding GPIO header pin must not be driven externally.

Correction to earlier session discussion: J11 connects to SER_CLK_N/T13;
J12 connects to SER_CLK/T12, the positive/single-ended reference input.
For single-ended external clock feedback, connect J8 to J12, not J11.
J12 must be populated. Leave the unused oscillator X3 path disconnected.
Dedicated serdes mode selects PLL_REF_SEL=0. The other modes export the
clock without instantiating CC_SERDES, and their scaled serial datapaths
continue to use the existing clocks.

Schematics: DS1003, pages 22, 33 and 34:
 https://colognechip.com/docs/ds1003-gatemate1-evb-3v1-a1-schematics-latest.pdf
 https://colognechip.com/docs/ds1003-gatemate1-evb-3v2-a1-schematics-latest.pdf

Verified CRG elaboration and generated IO_SB_A7/SLEW=fast constraints with
24/48 MHz system clocks in GMII and serial-loopback modes. Isolated Yosys
synthesis passed and retained both the system PLL and 125 MHz output PLL.
GateMate place-and-route, output frequency/jitter and SERDES lock require
verification with the actual toolchain and hardware. In particular, generating
125 MHz does not establish compliance with the SERDES reference-jitter limit.

==GateMate dedicated SERDES: 125 MHz GMII / 1.25 Gbit/s 1000BASE-X==

From systems/litex, with the normal LiteX environment/toolchain active:
 python3 -m boards.targets.colognechip_gatemate_evb \
   --toolchain peppercorn --cpu-type vexriscv --cpu-variant linux \
   --bus-standard axi-lite --network-tester-mode serdes --build

For near-end PMA loopback inside the physical transceiver, additionally use:
 --network-tester-serdes-loopback

The dedicated mode requires the J8 -> J12 reference-clock cable and board
setup described above, even when using the internal PMA loopback. Without
--network-tester-serdes-loopback, connect the dedicated SERDES pins to the
external link. Board connectors are J16=TX+, J15=TX-, J14=RX+, J13=RX-;
for a physical cable loopback, connect J16 -> J14 and J15 -> J13. Populate
missing connectors and verify the board's reference-clock electrical/jitter
requirements before use. Generic --network-tester-serial-*-pin arguments do
not apply to this mode. No SFP-specific driver-swing preset is claimed.

Path:
 generator 0 (125 MHz GMII) -> existing TX PCS / 8b10b encoder -> TX gearbox
 -> CC_SERDES (20-bit raw interface at 62.5 MHz) -> dedicated serial pair
 -> hardware CDR / comma alignment -> RX gearbox -> parallel RX PCS
 -> analyzer 0 (125 MHz recovered-clock GMII).

The SERDES ADPLL uses the external 125 MHz clock: N1=1, N2=2, N3=5, M3=2
produce a 625 MHz DDR bit clock for a 1.25 Gbit/s line. M1=10 and M4=1
produce a 62.5 MHz 20-bit interface (PLL_MAIN_DIVSEL=25, PLL_OUT_DIVSEL=1,
PLL_FCNTRL=26). TX/RX interface widths are 20 raw bits, native 8b10b is bypassed,
and the FPGA retains the project's encoding/decoding. Raw TX data is repeated
in all four 20-bit quarters of the native input bus, with DISPVAL providing
bit 8 and DISPMODE bit 9 per symbol; RX delivers contiguous raw code groups.
The raw bit order is reversed relative to the project's bit-9-first encoder.

Each interface clock is divided by two to a 31.25 MHz reference; separate
fabric PLLs then multiply by four to 125 MHz. This keeps their reference
inputs below CC_PLL's specified 50 MHz ceiling. Including the system PLL and the J8 clock-output PLL, this uses all
four fabric PLLs on the A1. With REGISTER_SYMBOL=1, the TX PCS registers
the selected byte/K/idle marker, then uses nine lookup cycles. First-level
banks have local copies of bits 0 and 1; later stages resolve the remaining
address bits through registered decisions.
Both disparity variants are precomputed; live running disparity selects the
final result. Idle variant selection happens at the output so that it follows
the preceding comma despite pipeline latency. PCS encoder latency is ten GMII
cycles (80 ns at 125 MHz), with one symbol per cycle throughput. Lookup
and tag payload registers are reset-free; local selector-flop copies serve
banks of up to four lookup entries, without adding latency. Process-level
keep attributes preserve the copies through synthesis. The validity chain masks
them with a known comma until the pipeline has refilled, reducing reset fanout.
TX also registers the encoder output and packs two
consecutive symbols into a 20-bit register, with no TX gearbox RAM/FIFO. Its
62.5 MHz word clock is divided directly from the 125 MHz GMII clock; words
change on falling edges and the SERDES samples rising edges. The divider
runs during datapath reset so the native TX reset sequence can complete.
RX retains its asynchronous FIFO and prefill. The low-fanout
SERDES half-rate clocks use local routing, with clkbuf_inhibit attributes,
while fabric PLL outputs supply the main global clocks. The system clock and
the previous modes keep their existing frequencies. RX packet processing
resets on loss of native alignment and waits for PCS synchronization.
External TX can start independently of RX; PMA-loopback TX waits for RX sync.

This is forced-link 1000BASE-X, with no Clause 37 auto-negotiation controller.
Configure the link partner accordingly. Hardware signal/clock conditioning
and timing closure must be established on the actual board/toolchain.
The time buses use coherent sys-to-GMII snapshot handshakes, rather than
sampling fast GMII edges in the slower system clock domain. Timestamp values
can lag the system counter and are not calibrated physical latency readings.
Existing generator/analyzer CSR names/order and register programming apply.

Validation status for this mode: 25 LiteX/serial regression tests passed,
including digital CC_SERDES/CC_PLL interface models at 125 MHz, valid-FCS
packet reception with 8/32-bit CSRs, ordered control writes, and loss of RX
alignment followed by reset/retraining. The final 31.25 MHz-reference PLL
revision also passed both dedicated-mode tests. These models validate digital
interfaces only; they do not model analog CDR, PLL jitter or SFP eye compliance.

Full SoC gateware generation and Yosys synthesis succeeded. With the installed
OSS CAD Suite, nextpnr router2 failed routing a logic net; a router1/seed-2
retry advanced too slowly and was interrupted. No routed bitstream or 125 MHz
timing closure is claimed. Build logs are /tmp/gatemate-serdes-final-build.log
and /tmp/gatemate-serdes-route1.log. The tested synthesis/routing configuration
used a standard VexRiscv and 32 KiB integrated main RAM; Linux/HyperRAM builds
may require additional routing/timing work. The installed timing model defaults
to SPEED and warns about the existing ECONOMY PLL configuration; match the
actual board supply/performance mode when validating timing. Hardware operation
has not been tested and the FPGA has not been programmed.

==Smaller CPU configuration for SERDES bring-up==

The CPU only runs the BIOS/CSR control plane; packet generation and reception
remain autonomous 125 MHz hardware. A PicoRV32 minimal configuration, a single
Wishbone CPU bus and internal RAM reduce routing pressure:

 python3 -m boards.targets.colognechip_gatemate_evb \
   --toolchain peppercorn --cpu-type picorv32 --cpu-variant minimal \
   --bus-standard wishbone \
   --integrated-rom-size 0x10000 --integrated-sram-size 0x2000 \
   --integrated-main-ram-size 0x8000 \
   --network-tester-mode serdes --network-tester-serdes-loopback \
   --output-dir build/colognechip_gatemate_evb_picorv32 \
   --csr-csv build/colognechip_gatemate_evb_picorv32/csr.csv --build

Remove --network-tester-serdes-loopback to use an external serial link.
Use this configuration's generated csr.csv with the serial Python tests.
The 32 KiB internal main RAM avoids the HyperRAM controller; the BIOS needs
about 25.5 KiB ROM and 2.8 KiB SRAM in the tested environment. This CPU runs
bare-metal firmware, not Linux. This profile does not change the target defaults.

With compiled BIOS, source generation, Yosys synthesis and nextpnr packing
succeeded. Packed LT usage was 22,434/40,960 (54%) and FF usage 7,113/40,960
(17%), versus 26,424 (64%) and 8,262 (20%) for the prior VexRiscv standard/AXI
bring-up configuration. This compares complete build configurations, not just
CPU implementations; bus and memory/firmware configurations also differ.
All four PLLs remain used, and the 125 MHz datapath timing still needs checking.

Transmit-only dedicated SERDES mode (GateMate)
--------------------------------------------
Use --network-tester-mode serdes_tx to instantiate only the traffic generator
and the 125 MHz GMII / 1.25 Gbit/s SERDES transmit path. No traffic analyzer,
receive PCS, RX gearbox, recovered-clock PLL, or RX clock domains are instantiated;
the hard SERDES receiver is powered down. Generator CSR names are unchanged;
analyzer CSRs are absent. This mode uses three fabric PLLs including the system
clock and J8 reference output. The external J8-to-J12 reference-clock connection
is still required. --network-tester-serdes-loopback is unavailable in this mode.

Example with a small processor and internal RAM:

    python3 -m boards.targets.colognechip_gatemate_evb \
        --toolchain peppercorn --cpu-type picorv32 --cpu-variant minimal \
        --bus-standard wishbone --integrated-rom-size 0x10000 \
        --integrated-sram-size 0x2000 --integrated-main-ram-size 0x8000 \
        --network-tester-mode serdes_tx \
        --output-dir build/colognechip_gatemate_evb_serdes_tx \
        --csr-csv build/colognechip_gatemate_evb_serdes_tx/csr.csv --build

Dummy PCS transmit mode (GateMate)
---------------------------------
Use --network-tester-mode serdes_dummy_pcs_tx for the generator and dedicated
SERDES TX path without the 1000BASE-X PCS or 8b/10b encoder. Each GMII byte
becomes {2'b00, gmii_data[7:0]}. The native SERDES sends bits 0 through 9,
i.e. the byte least-significant bit first, followed by two zero bits. Every
GMII cycle produces a symbol, including idle cycles; gmii_en and gmii_er
are ignored. This raw test stream is not valid 1000BASE-X.

The register TX gearbox, 125 MHz GMII clock, 62.5 MHz word clock and
1.25 Gbit/s dedicated transmitter remain. No analyzer or RX logic is
instantiated, and the hardware receiver stays powered down. Generator CSRs
are unchanged. The J8-to-J12 reference clock connection is still required;
PMA loopback and serial GPIO pin options are unavailable in this mode.

    python3 -m boards.targets.colognechip_gatemate_evb \
        --toolchain peppercorn --cpu-type picorv32 --cpu-variant minimal \
        --bus-standard wishbone --integrated-rom-size 0x10000 \
        --integrated-sram-size 0x2000 --integrated-main-ram-size 0x8000 \
        --network-tester-mode serdes_dummy_pcs_tx \
        --output-dir build/colognechip_gatemate_evb_dummy_pcs_tx \
        --csr-csv build/colognechip_gatemate_evb_dummy_pcs_tx/csr.csv --build

Control CSR crossing queue timing
--------------------------------
The 16-entry control queues use registered binary RAM addresses and Gray
pointers synchronized between sys and the datapath clock. Full/empty flags
are registered separately. The RAM read output is latched (READ_FIRST),
with a matching valid bit, so the slot can safely be reused after its read.
No synchronized pointer, empty comparison, or next-address increment logic
feeds the RAM read-address input directly. Once data is available, queued
writes drain at one control update per datapath cycle. Overflow remains
sticky until system reset; datapath reset discards queued commands and
restores the current CSR value, as before.

Fixed 64/20 frame transmit mode (GateMate)
---------------------------------------
Use --network-tester-mode serdes_64data_20gap_tx for the CSR-free
traffic_generator_64data_20gap_gmii core feeding the same 1000BASE-X PCS,
8b/10b encoder, register gearbox and dedicated transmitter as serdes_tx.
It runs at 125 MHz GMII / 1.25 Gbit/s serial. No configurable traffic core,
control FIFO, analyzer, timestamp crossings or rtclock is instantiated.
The fixed generator starts automatically once the TX path is ready, with
one startup idle cycle to prime its inferred block ROM. It emits
8 preamble/SFD octets, a 64-octet frame including FCS, then 12 idle clocks:
84 GMII clocks per frame. No traffic CSR writes are needed or available.
The J8-to-J12 reference-clock connection remains necessary.

    python3 -m boards.targets.colognechip_gatemate_evb \
        --toolchain peppercorn --cpu-type picorv32 --cpu-variant minimal \
        --bus-standard wishbone --integrated-rom-size 0x10000 \
        --integrated-sram-size 0x2000 --integrated-main-ram-size 0x8000 \
        --network-tester-mode serdes_64data_20gap_tx \
        --output-dir build/colognechip_gatemate_evb_64data_20gap_tx \
        --csr-csv build/colognechip_gatemate_evb_64data_20gap_tx/csr.csv --build --no-compile-gateware

For the validated PCS-symbol register / local-copy implementation, synthesize
and route with the following settings after generating the above build:

    cd build/colognechip_gatemate_evb_64data_20gap_tx/gateware
    yosys -Q -l colognechip_gatemate_evb.rpt colognechip_gatemate_evb.ys
    nextpnr-himbaechel --json colognechip_gatemate_evb.json \
        --device CCGM1A1 --vopt ccf=colognechip_gatemate_evb.ccf \
        --vopt out=colognechip_gatemate_evb.txt --router router2 \
        --seed 3 --freq 125 --timing-allow-fail \
        --placer-heap-timingweight 40 --tmg-ripup \
        --log timing.log --report timing.json
    gmpack colognechip_gatemate_evb.txt colognechip_gatemate_evb.bit

The recorded FINAL routed result was 128.52 MHz for GMII (required 125 MHz)
and 28.24 MHz for sys (required 24 MHz). The default routing settings did not
close timing. Check the final routed report before using the bitstream;
--timing-allow-fail permits completion even when timing fails. The result uses
nextpnr's SPEED/WORST model, with the existing PLL ECONOMY/SPEED mismatch
warnings. Tool settings and board performance configuration matter.
The input netlist, constraints, reports and passing bitstream are archived
under codex/reports/pcs-symbol-register at the project root.


GateMate console through the onboard USB connector
-------------------------------------------------
Add --console jtag to the GateMate build command to use the onboard USB
JTAG connection for the LiteX console. The default --console pmod retains
the existing external USB-UART adapter on PMODB. A normal /dev/ttyUSB port
is not the console for the JTAG build. No EEPROM changes are required.

GateMate has no native LiteX JTAGPHY backend. This build reclaims the WA
configuration pins after loading and implements a fabric TAP, with a
6-bit instruction register, IDCODE 0x20000001, USER instruction 0x02 and
LiteX's ten-bit console stream framing. Configuration status pins are
held at their successful-load levels while the fabric is running.
The pin mapping follows the GateMate EVB 3.2/A1 schematic:
https://colognechip.com/docs/ds1003-gatemate1-evb-3v2-a1-schematics-latest.pdf
The host clock is limited to 1 MHz. Runtime configuration instructions
are unavailable in the fabric TAP; openFPGALoader asserts configuration
reset before loading, restoring the hard configuration TAP. Close the
console before programming because both tools use FT2232 channel A.

Generate a separate build so the previously validated bitstream is kept:

    python3 -m boards.targets.colognechip_gatemate_evb \
        --toolchain peppercorn --cpu-type picorv32 --cpu-variant minimal \
        --bus-standard wishbone --integrated-rom-size 0x10000 \
        --integrated-sram-size 0x2000 --integrated-main-ram-size 0x8000 \
        --network-tester-mode serdes_64data_20gap_tx --console jtag \
        --output-dir build/colognechip_gatemate_evb_jtag \
        --csr-csv build/colognechip_gatemate_evb_jtag/csr.csv \
        --build --no-compile-gateware

For this console version, use the following validated routing settings
(seed 2, default placement timing weight, timing-driven rip-up):

    cd build/colognechip_gatemate_evb_jtag/gateware
    yosys -Q -l synthesis.log colognechip_gatemate_evb.ys
    nextpnr-himbaechel --json colognechip_gatemate_evb.json \
        --device CCGM1A1 --vopt ccf=colognechip_gatemate_evb.ccf \
        --vopt out=colognechip_gatemate_evb.txt --router router2 \
        --seed 2 --freq 125 --tmg-ripup --timing-allow-fail \
        --sdc ../../../prog/gatemate_console.sdc \
        --log timing.log --report timing.json
    gmpack colognechip_gatemate_evb.txt colognechip_gatemate_evb.bit

FINAL routed timing: GMII 126.49 MHz PASS125, sys 26.34 MHz PASS24,
JTAG 44.04 MHz PASS1. The half-rate clock is conservatively checked at the
125 MHz global default, as in the earlier PMODB-console run. These settings
apply to this netlist; the prior 128.52 MHz result belongs to the PMODB build.
Check the final report: --timing-allow-fail does not establish timing closure.
Existing PLL ECONOMY/SPEED warnings remain; the model is SPEED/WORST.

The JTAG target automatically includes prog/gatemate_console.sdc in the
generated Peppercorn build script, so TCK is checked at the host's 1 MHz.
Manual nextpnr invocations must include it too. No traffic clock was lowered.
All 33 LiteX tests passed. The final bitstream was loaded on the EVB 3.2A
and Enter/help were tested using the actual litex_term command. Reports,
reproduction inputs, hardware logs and bitstream: codex/reports/jtag-console.

From systems/litex, program the new bitstream and connect:

    openFPGALoader -b gatemate_evb_jtag \
        build/colognechip_gatemate_evb_jtag/gateware/colognechip_gatemate_evb.bit
    litex_term jtag --jtag-config prog/openocd_gatemate_console.cfg

OpenOCD must be on PATH. The supplied configuration normalizes multi-field
drscan whitespace for compatibility with this LiteX/OpenOCD combination.
Press Enter for the BIOS prompt. --speed is not
needed: JTAG transfer speed is set by adapter speed in the OpenOCD config.
The generator still requires the J8-to-J12 reference-clock cable.


Versa ECP5: internally clocked 1000BASE-X on the SMA pairs
-------------------------------------------------------
Requires LiteICLink alongside the standard LiteX/Trellis tools.
The lattice_versa_ecp5 target supports the dedicated SERDES modes on the
LFE5UM and LFE5UM5G devices. The default system clock remains 75 MHz and
the normal board UART remains enabled; no JTAG console is added.
An additional PLL output derives the 125 MHz SERDES reference from the
onboard 100 MHz LVDS oscillator. No external reference-clock cable is needed.
The DCU PLL uses 125 MHz x20 /2 for a 1.25 Gbit/s serial line rate. The DCU
runs its native 10-bit interface at 125 MHz, with its 1:2 gearing disabled;
there is no fabric gearbox or RX elastic FIFO in this wrapper. The ECP5
RX selects REGISTER_DECODE=1 in the shared parallel PCS. Its RD-independent
8b/10b lookup uses one synchronous block ROM, with the code/valid tag delayed
to match. This adds one 125 MHz cycle of receive latency while preserving
one code group per cycle. The existing combinational decoder remains the
default for other targets. Regenerate/check the table with
python3 examples/generate_8b10b_rx_rom.py [--check].

The existing platform resources select DCU0 channel 1:

    Direction   Resource   SMA       FPGA ball
    TX positive sma_tx.p   J7        W8
    TX negative sma_tx.n   J8        W9
    RX positive sma_rx.p   J5        Y7
    RX negative sma_rx.n   J6        Y8

For an external electrical loopback use two suitable SMA cables: J7 to J5,
and J8 to J6. These are differential serial-data pairs. In particular J8
is TX negative on the Versa board. The PCIe channel is not used.

The new serdes_64data_20gap mode combines the fixed 64-octet generator with
traffic_analyzer_gmii_0 on RX. The generator starts automatically after the
local TX PLL is ready. The analyzer is enabled by default and exposes its
usual CSR counters and capture registers. Its frame_size reports 72 for
this stream because that register includes eight preamble/SFD octets;
the Ethernet frame including FCS remains 64 octets. The 20-cycle interval
contains eight preamble/SFD octets and twelve idle octets.

TX starts and continues with RX disconnected. RX loss asserts the analyzer
reset and RX re-acquires when the signal returns; statistics restart on RX
reset. The RT clock core runs on the 125 MHz TX clock, preserving its 8 ns
tick, with coherent snapshots into the recovered RX clock domain. CSR
access and the CPU remain on the system clock.

Other supported modes retain the shared meanings:
- serdes: CSR-configurable generator plus RX analyzer.
- serdes_tx: CSR-configurable generator, TX only.
- serdes_64data_20gap_tx: fixed generator, TX only.
- serdes_dummy_pcs_tx: raw diagnostic TX pattern.
The ECP5 wrapper does not implement --network-tester-serdes-loopback;
use the external SMA cables for loopback instead. As with the GateMate
wrapper, the PCS uses forced-link 1000BASE-X without auto-negotiation.

Build the fixed generator plus RX analyzer from systems/litex:

    python3 -m boards.targets.lattice_versa_ecp5 \
        --toolchain trellis --device LFE5UM5G \
        --cpu-type picorv32 --cpu-variant minimal --bus-standard wishbone \
        --integrated-rom-size 0x10000 --integrated-sram-size 0x2000 \
        --integrated-main-ram-size 0x8000 \
        --network-tester-mode serdes_64data_20gap \
        --output-dir build/lattice_versa_ecp5_1000basex \
        --csr-csv build/lattice_versa_ecp5_1000basex/csr.csv \
        --build --no-compile-gateware

Then synthesize and route with the validated placement region:

    cd build/lattice_versa_ecp5_1000basex/gateware
    yosys -Q -l synthesis.log lattice_versa_ecp5.ys
    nextpnr-ecp5 --json lattice_versa_ecp5.json --lpf lattice_versa_ecp5.lpf \
        --textcfg lattice_versa_ecp5.config --um5g-45k --package CABGA381 \
        --speed 8 --seed 1 --router router1 --timing-allow-fail \
        --pre-place ../../../prog/versa_ecp5_sequence_region.py \
        --log timing.log --report timing.json
    ecppack --bootaddr 0 lattice_versa_ecp5.config \
        --svf lattice_versa_ecp5.svf --bit lattice_versa_ecp5.bit

HARDWARE VALIDATION CORRECTION (2026-11-09): the earlier archived design
had an empty CPU boot ROM after gateware regeneration with
--no-compile-software. It cannot boot the BIOS. Always compile software as
in the command above, or provide --integrated-rom-init <matching bios.bin>
when skipping software compilation. Verify the generated *_rom.init is
nonempty before synthesis. The previously reported timing below applies
to that incomplete netlist and must be revalidated with BIOS included.

The FINAL post-route result for this LFE5UM5G-45F-8 configuration is:
RX 132.82 MHz PASS125, TX 220.31 MHz PASS125, system 83.55 MHz PASS75.
The placement region keeps 526 sequence-comparison and error-counter cells
near one another; the analyzer Verilog is unchanged. Default unconstrained
placement missed RX timing, so the region is part of this validated recipe.
The earlier placement estimate failed; only the final routed report passes.
--timing-allow-fail permits completion and does not establish timing closure.
For LFE5UM, use --device LFE5UM and --um-45k; that variant needs its own
routing/timing validation. Other network-tester modes also need separate
physical timing validation. The bitstream has not been tested on hardware.
Inputs, final reports, CSR map, bitstream and SVF are archived under
codex/reports/versa-1000basex at the project root.

Use --network-tester-mode serdes for a configurable generator instead.
Use the generated csr.csv addresses for crg_traffic_analyzer_gmii_0_pkts,
crg_traffic_analyzer_gmii_0_bad_crc_pkts and other analyzer registers.
The normal console is litex_term <board-UART-device> --speed 115200.

Implementation references: LiteICLink serdes_ecp5.py supplies the DCUA
analog/electrical parameter profile; this project supplies initialization,
raw 10-bit datapaths and its existing Verilog TX/RX PCS. LiteX-Boards'
lattice_versa_ecp5 platform supplies the SMA constraints. The digital
simulation tests cover frames/CRC, absent RX, clock loss/recovery and the
125 MHz timestamp tick; they do not model analog CDR, jitter or eye quality.


GateMate full-receive ROM/placement experiment
--------------------------------------------
The native GateMate receive path now enables the registered 8b/10b block-ROM
decoder described above. It maps to one CC_BRAM_20K and adds one RX cycle.
40 LiteX tests passed, including CRC/frame reception, alignment recovery,
and both native CSR bus widths. The TX-only path is unchanged.

Full TX/RX uses all four PLL sites. With --console jtag, the 1 MHz TAP clock
is routed in fabric to avoid a fifth PLL-backed global clock buffer. The
existing transmit-only JTAG clock-buffer configuration is unchanged.

The tested mode was serdes_64data_20gap, at the existing 24 MHz system clock
and125 MHz GMII. Baseline, sequence-region and CP-clock-routing attempts
all failed routing. NO timing-closed full-receive image has been generated.
Placement estimates in those logs must not be read as final routed timing.
The experimental script and exact failing-run inputs/reports are under
codex/reports/gatemate-rx-rom. The analyzer Verilog is unchanged.
